

  <section xml:id="Math2301HW6">
    <title>Calculus Eigen</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-power-series">
      <title>Calc 2: Power Series</title>

      <p>
        In Calc <m>2</m>, you learn about power series. They look like this:
        <me>
          f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_{n}x^{n}.
        </me>
        Taylor series tells you that any sufficiently nice function has a power series representation given by
        <me>
          f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}x^{n}.
        </me>
        Thus, they are a useful model of functions.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-power-series-coefficients">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let <m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_{n} x^{n}</m> be an infinitely differentiable function.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that
              <me>
                f^{(n)}(0) = n!\, a_{n},
              </me>
              where <m>f^{(n)}</m> is the <m>n</m>th derivative of <m>f</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that if <m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} b_{n} x^{n}</m>, then <m>a_{n} = b_{n}</m> for all <m>n \geq 0</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        Power series are useful for describing eigenvectors of the derivative.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-derivative-eigenvectors">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let <m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_{n} x^{n}</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find a power series for <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that if <m>\frac{df}{dx} = \lambda f(x)</m>, then <m>\lambda a_{n} = (n+1)a_{n+1}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Explain why this means the set of solutions to the differential equation <m>\frac{df}{dx} = \lambda f(x)</m> is <m>1</m>-dimensional.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Describe the set of solutions to <m>\frac{df}{dx} = f(x)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Describe the set of all eigenvectors of the derivative.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-second-derivatives">
      <title>Multivariable Calc: Second Derivatives</title>

      <p>
        For a function <m>f: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}</m>, its <term>gradient</term> is the function <m>\nabla f: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{n}</m> given by <m>(\nabla f(\vec{x}))_{i} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_{i}}</m>.
        Its <term>Hessian</term> is <m>H_{f}: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}</m>, which is the <m>n \times n</m> matrix given by
        <me>
          H_{f}(\vec{x})_{i, j} = \frac{\partial^{2}f(\vec{x})}{\partial x_{i} \partial x_{j}}.
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        The following collection of exercises answers the question: Which matrices can appear as Hessians?
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix. Then the quadratic form associated with <m>A</m> is
        <me>
          q_{A}(\vec{x}) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} A_{i,j} x_{i} x_{j}.
        </me>
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-quadratic-form-partials">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix. Show that for all <m>1 \leq i \leq n</m>:
            <me>
              \frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}} q_{A}(\vec{x}) = \sum_{j = 1}^{n}(A_{j,i} + A_{i,j})x_{j}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-quadratic-form-hessian">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that
            <me>
              H_{q_{A}}(\vec{x}) = A+A^{\intercal}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-symmetric-hessian">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that any symmetric matrix is the Hessian of some quadratic form.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>
